# BUG-245 regression fixture — the Shell.run CODE defect (the doc half is in QUICKSTART).
#
# Two independent faults, both pinned here because either alone breaks the namespace:
#
#   1. The emitter called `std.process.Child.run(.{...})`, removed in Zig 0.16. The
#      allocator and Io are POSITIONAL now (`std.process.run(_allocator, _io, .{...})`) and
#      `max_output_bytes` no longer exists. If that regresses, this file will not compile.
#
#   2. `Shell.run` had no TypeChecker arm, so its result typed as unresolved and NO str
#      method would dispatch on it -- `.contains` failed with "no field or member function
#      named 'contains' in 'str'" even though the value was a string. Calling a str method
#      on the result is therefore the assertion, not incidental style.
#
# Third instance of the BUG-241/242 pattern: a namespace with no run coverage was never
# exercised, so its Zig 0.16 migration never happened and nothing could find out.
def main()
    var out = Shell.run("echo bug245")

    # Fault 2: a str method must DISPATCH on the result at all.
    assert out.contains("bug245"), "Shell.run result is a usable str"

    # Fault 1 is pinned by this file compiling and the child actually running: a stale
    # std.process.Child.run would fail the Zig build before reaching any assertion.
    assert out.len > 0, "child process produced output"

    print("bug245: OK")
